when you think of english-speaking  countries one of the countries that  comes to mind might be new zealand  new zealand is indeed an  english-speaking country but there's  another official language there  one that was there long before the  arrival of english speakers  that language is maori or  the maori language maori is an eastern  polynesian language which belongs to the  wider austronesian language family  it's closely related to hawaiian  hawaiian is part of the markasic branch  of eastern polynesian  and maori is part of the tahitic branch  so it's very similar to the tahitian  language  maori became an official language of new  zealand in 1987  but being an official language doesn't  mean that it's spoken by a large portion  of the population  according to the 2013 census 125 000  people speak maori  or around 21 of the ethnic maori  population  and 3 percent of new zealand's  population but that number seems to  include people with varying degrees of  ability in the language  it's also spoken by around 12 000 maori  immigrants in australia  that's about 8.2 percent of maori  immigrants there  before europeans began to settle there  in the late 1700s  maori was the predominant language  spoken in new zealand on both the  northern and southern islands  and for the first several decades of  settlement many europeans learned maori  but as more traders missionaries and  settlers arrived the number of english  speakers grew  and by the 1860s english speakers began  to outnumber maori speakers  this was partly a result of a major  decline in the maori population  due to diseases introduced by europeans  and the inter-tribal musket wars  beginning in 1867 the native schools act  established a system of primary schools  for maori children  conducted entirely in english this  contributed to the dominance of english  and the decline of maori but most maori  people  still spoke their traditional language  at home until many of them started  migrating to urban areas after the  second world war  a language revival movement gained  momentum in the 1980s and 90s  and in recent years there seems to be  more energy and interest behind revival  efforts once again  we'll look at some basic maori phrases  in a minute but first let's look at a  few features of maori pronunciation and  orthography  there are only 15 letters in the maori  alphabet 8 consonants and 5 vowels  the eight consonants include  and two digraphs no and wh  which is usually pronounced  a voiced bilabial fricative like an f  sound but with your lips touching  there are more variations than this but  these are the most common ones  the small number of consonants in maori  is quite striking when we look at  english loanwords like this one  baby there's no voiced ba  sound in maori so it becomes a voiceless  meaning sheep notice that there's no  sound in maori  so she becomes he the vowels include  and there's one phoneme per letter in  other words maori is written as it  sounds  diphthongs in maori are like vowel  sequences with both vowel sounds  remaining distinct  in other words diphthongs are all  composed of sounds from this list  though there is some variation in how  they're pronounced and for some there's  a generational difference  there's a phonemic distinction between  short vowels and long vowels  and long vowels are normally shown with  a macron for example  goku means shovel while koko  means tui a kind of bird all  syllables end in a vowel for example  meaning man meaning  meaning child there are no consonant  clusters  we can see that in the way this english  loanword is pronounced  for milk notice the extra vowel that's  inserted to break up this consonant  cluster  lk also notice that there's no le sound  in maori so it's pronounced as a  instead let's look at a few basic  phrases  as you see and listen to these phrases  notice the features of maori  pronunciation and orthography that i  talked about in the previous section  hello that's when you say hello to one  person  hello when said to two people  hello one said to three or more people  notice that in maori there's a  distinction between singular dual and  plural  we'll come back to this a little later  good morning  literally peaceful morning specific  greetings for other times of the day  exist  but aren't so commonly used and its dual  and plural forms are normally used  how are you this is the present  continuous aspect marker which expresses  what's happening right now  how you i'm fine  word for word it's present continuous  fine or good  i what's your name  word for word it's specifying particle  who  your name my name is paula  specifying particle paul my name  this word here ko is like a specifying  topic particle  which marks something specific when it  appears as the predicate  please tina thank you when said to one  person  to two people and to three or more  people  or more casually  let's look at the basic word order of  maori  this is a sentence without a verb but it  has a subject and a predicate  notice that the predicate comes first  and the subject comes after it  that's the opposite of the english order  this occurs in many austronesian  languages  including hawaiian and tagalog a  language spoken in the philippines  another example the baby is cute  word for word it's indefinite article  cute the baby  the predicate of this sentence is an  adjective as well as  hey the indefinite article which comes  before an adjective when it serves as a  predicate  this means the cute baby and is not a  full sentence but just a noun phrase  with an adjective  note that the adjective always comes  after the noun in a noun phrase like  this  the basic order in the sentences so far  has been predicate subject  but in sentences with verbs the basic  word order is vso  though there are some exceptions to this  which we might see later  i ate dinner word for word it's past  tense marker  eat i direct object marker the dinner  the word ahau is the first person  singular personal pronoun  the personal pronouns are used as both  subject pronouns and  object pronouns for example in this  sentence  which means she loves me or he loves me  ahau  is the object word for word this  sentence is present continuous marker or  right now  love he or she to me  a moment ago we saw the definite article  this is the singular definite article  the plural definite article is  the person the people note that this  vowel  becomes a long vowel in the plural form  there's also an indefinite article  hey a person the definite articles  basically refer to something specific  and the indefinite article basically  refers to something non-specific  but the articles don't exactly function  the same way they do in english  one difference is that hey is used  before an adjective when it's the  predicate of a sentence  we saw that in this sentence before  meaning the baby is cute notice that hey  comes before the adjective  also in many cases hay is not used and a  different determiner is used instead  it's a determiner which means ah or one  or a certain  something for plural nouns  we're going to see a movie word for word  it's non-past marker  c we direct object marker a  movie let's also look at the  demonstrative adjectives or  demonstrative pronouns which have a  three-way distinction in location  this dna dna  this book that when something is near  the listener  that book near the listener that when  something is away from both the speaker  and the listener  that book when it's away from both  people for the plural forms you simply  drop the t  at the beginning of the word these  these books those near the listener  those books near the listener those away  from both the speaker and the listener  those books over there  that is julia's book over there word for  word it's  specific particle definite article book  possessive particle  julia over there here's the  demonstrative pronoun  is the specifying particle we saw before  it's often used in equational sentences  with no verb  possessives ah is a particle that shows  possession  the possessor hurya comes after it and  the thing that's possessed  comes before it ah is one form of the  possessive particle  but it can also be o it depends on the  nature of the possession  ah is used when you can choose whether  you possess it or not  or you're dominant over it like a parent  possesses a child  those are a-class possessives  julia's book or is used when you can't  choose whether you possess it or not  or you are subordinate to the object  like a child possesses a parent these  are o-class possessives  for example julia's nose  julia can't really decide whether she  possesses her nose or not  these possessives have additional  alternate forms  in the plural the t is dropped  these possessive particles are also  added to the plural  and dual personal pronouns to form the  plural and dual possessive adjectives or  possessive pronouns  for example our book  inclusive our book  exclusive notice that in maori there's a  distinction between the first person  plural inclusive  which includes the listener and  exclusive which excludes the listener  and the dual possessive adjectives and  pronouns are formed in the same way  for example this means our book  inclusive but in this case our means  belonging to the two of us  because it's dual the singular  possessives  don't have the particles we saw above  but they do have  a class and o class forms as well as  neutral forms  for example means my book  when a possessive comes before a noun  the neutral form can optionally be used  instead of the a  form or o form now let's focus a little  bit on  verbs in maori there's no verb meaning  to be  we can see this in the sentence we saw  earlier  the baby is cute the pattern is simply  predicate subject  another important thing to point out is  that verbs in maori are not  conjugated for person and number the  verb form doesn't change regardless of  the subject  they sleep and so on on top of that  the verbs themselves are not conjugated  for tense  instead there are verbal particles  mostly before the verb that indicate  time aspect or mood you can see one  example of those verbal particles in  these sentences here  e this represents past tense  i learned for past continuous  i was learning for present continuous  i'm learning for perfect aspect  i have learned or i had learned notice  that this doesn't tell us the tense of  the verb but that the action has been  completed  this is used for non-past in other words  the general present tense or the future  and the inceptive form inceptive means  it shows the beginning of an action  i learn or i'll learn  this indicates the imperfect or  continuous aspect  i'm learning and so on now let's look at  a couple more sentences and break them  down  this means we don't live in the city we  live in the countryside  word for word it's not we exclusive  imperfect live in the town big  in the countryside we exclusive  imperfect live first we see the negation  word  there are a few different negation words  depending on the type of sentence  but this is the most common one with a  verb in an indicative sentence  here it's followed by the first person  plural exclusive pronoun  meaning that it excludes the listener  here we see one of the verbal particles  or  verbal particle units that we saw a few  moments ago here it expresses the  imperfect  to indicate that a state is still  ongoing notice that this is a particle  with two components which  are split by the verb which means live  is a preposition meaning at or in and  the phrase  means the big town but notice again that  the adjective comes after the noun  remember before when i said that maori  is generally vso or predicate subject  well in a sentence like this with a  negative the word order changes  after the negation word the subject  comes before the verb  notice that the second sentence begins  with  a phrase showing location whereas the  phrase showing location in the first  sentence comes at the end  this phrase appears at the beginning of  the sentence to place emphasis on it  as you can see maori is for the most  part a straightforward language  as well as a fun language to delve into  but it does remain an endangered  language  in recent years there have been  inspiring attempts to promote and  revitalize the language  and those efforts continue today in fact  the current prime minister of new  zealand  is aiming for 1 million new maori  speakers by the year 2040.  she also intends to raise her daughter  to be bilingual in  english and maori and several elements  of the education system aim to increase  the number of fluent maori speakers  one element is kohangareo which  literally means  language nest this is a type of early  childhood education facility where the  children are immersed in maori language  and culture  there are also maori immersion schools  and bilingual schools  but only a minority of maori students  study in these schools  around 20 percent at the kohangareo  level and 10 percent in primary and  secondary schools  whether or not maori can be successfully  revitalized really depends on enrollment  in these programs  as a lover of languages i really hope  that the maori language will survive and  thrive the question of the day if you're  of maori origin  how much of the maori language do you  know in what situations do you use it  and for other people are there any  similarly endangered languages in your  country  are there any efforts to revitalize them  leave your answers in the comments down  below  and it's time to give special thanks to  all of the langfocus patreon supporters  who make it much easier to keep this  channel going month after month  i especially want to thank the ones  whose names appear right here on the  screen because they are the top tier  patreon supporters so many extra special  thanks go out to them  and everyone thank you for watching and  see ya  [Music]  you

